### NATS — the RPC bus, via its HTTP monitoring port
###
### NATS itself speaks its own protocol, but the server exposes a read-only
### HTTP monitoring API. That is the closest you can get to "watching the RPC
### layer" from a .http file, and it is genuinely useful: /subsz shows which
### subjects each service is currently answering.
###
### Requires: pnpm dc:up:infra

@nats = http://localhost:8222

### Server overview — uptime, connection count, slow consumers
GET {{nats}}/varz

### JetStream status. THIS PROJECT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT USE JETSTREAM —
### durability is Kafka's job (see the Kafka-vs-NATS decision matrix).
### Expect: { "disabled": true }
GET {{nats}}/jsz

### Current connections — one per running service, plus any test client
GET {{nats}}/connz

### SUBSCRIPTIONS — the interesting one. With orders, fulfillment and billing
### running you should see the saga's subjects being answered:
###   orders.create, fulfillment.stock.check, fulfillment.stock.reserve,
###   fulfillment.stock.release, fulfillment.despatch.create,
###   billing.credit.hold, billing.credit.list, ...
GET {{nats}}/subsz?subs=1

### Routing/gateway info (single-node here, so mostly empty)
GET {{nats}}/routez
